The build gate exists because a number that looked like a measurement was not one, and the record had no way to tell the difference until it was too late. The incident that forced the gate is recorded as INC-007, and understanding it is the fastest way to understand why the build script does what it does today.

The trouble began with Table 1. The table reports wall-clock totals across the instrument runs, broken into four per-wave maxima. Those maxima are 43, 109, 525 and 163 seconds, and they sum to 840. For one revision the table carried a total of 860 seconds instead. The reader who wants to reproduce the record can already see the mismatch by adding the four numbers by hand, and that is exactly the point: the correct total was recoverable from the file itself, and nothing about the printed 860 could survive that arithmetic.

What matters for the record is not the arithmetic slip itself but where it came from. The 860 was never computed in the repository. It appeared in a reviewer's text, as a number the reviewer wrote down while reading. The review chair then carried the same number into the required-change list, treating it as an obligation to fix. The author, working from that list, copied the number into the table without recomputing it from the four maxima that were sitting in the same instrument data. So a transcription error travelled through three hands, each step conferring a little more apparent authority, until it landed in the paper's own table as if it were a measured value. The gate is the answer to that: no reviewer-supplied number, no chair-supplied number, and no author-supplied memory of either may enter a Table 1 cell by hand.

The fix is enforced mechanically rather than by good intentions. The PDF build script now runs a dedicated script, check_table1.sh, as a hard step in the build. That script recomputes every instrument-derived cell of Table 1 from the instrument table itself. The per-wave byte counts, the wall-clock maxima, the summed total of 840, and the resolver row are all regenerated from source data and compared against what the document currently states. Any mismatch fails the build, which means a wrong number can no longer ship; the build simply will not complete until the table agrees with the instruments. The locale is pinned during the check so that number formatting stays stable across machines, which removes an entire class of borderline failures where two machines disagree on how a value is printed and the gate trips on formatting rather than on truth. From the author's side this is a deliberate loss of convenience: a number cannot be pasted in and trusted, it has to survive recomputation or the build stops.

The gate is deliberately scoped, and the scoping is part of the honesty of the system. It covers only the instrument-derived cells, the ones that any reader could in principle recompute from the files. There is a second class of cells in Table 1 that the gate does not, and cannot, cover. The dispatch count N, the admitted characters, and the admitted fraction come from the hub's private store, a source no reader (and in practice no rebuild) can reach. Those hub-audited cells cannot be recomputed by anyone outside the hub, so they fall outside the mechanical check by necessity. What the incident log records frankly is that those excluded cells are precisely the ones that have failed most often. The gate therefore does not claim to make the table trustworthy in total; it claims to make the part that is independently verifiable verifiable, and to keep the unverifiable part visibly separate. That separation is itself a statement about where the record's certainty ends.

The incident also produced a lesson that goes beyond the build script and into how the author handles numbers in general. It is recorded in the incident log this way: a reviewer's number is a provenance lead, not provenance. A number that appears in a review tells the author where to look; it does not tell the author what the value of something is. The author re-derives from the files every time, rather than adopting the reviewer's version on strength of who supplied it. This rule was put to work quickly, and the incident log records the results as two later addenda. Together they document four more reviewer-supplied sentences that were transcribed without recomputation, meaning the same failure mode recurred after INC-007 established the rule. One of those sentences is worth describing because its failure is subtle: a per-brief range that was stated over twelve briefs but was actually true of only ten. The range was not wrong in a way that arithmetic would catch; over the full set of twelve it simply did not hold, and had it been checked against the briefs it would have come apart. That it survived shows why the rule is stated as re-derive from the files, not re-check the number. A sentence can carry a false scope that no single number alone reveals.

A second, related defect sits in the revision log rather than in the table, and it rounds out the record of how transcription fails. A later revision-log entry claimed that the committed file contained a dispatcher-header sentence. It did not. The claim described a post-state that was asserted from the fix list rather than from the diff: someone wrote the log entry as if the change had been made, when the file had not been changed to match. Three reviewers independently caught it by opening the file and looking, which is the cheap and decisive check, and the episode was recorded as an addendum to an earlier incident, INC-003, whose lesson is the same shape: a post-state asserted from a fix list, not from a diff. The rule that came out of it is that every revision-log line is checked against the diff before the tag is cut. The log is not allowed to describe a state of the tree that the tree does not actually hold.

Read together, the gate, the two lessons, and the two addenda describe a consistent policy. Numbers and sentences that originate outside the repository are treated as leads to be verified against the files, never as facts to be copied. Where verification is possible, it is made mechanical and it blocks the build. Where verification is impossible, because the source lives in the hub's private store, the record says so openly and flags those cells as the historically least reliable. And the log itself, which records all of this, is held to the same standard as the table: what it claims about the tree must match the tree, verified against the diff at tag time.

For the reader who wants to reproduce the record, the practical takeaway is simple. To confirm Table 1, add the four per-wave maxima by hand; the sum of 43, 109, 525 and 163 is 840, and any total other than that should be treated as a transcription failure. To confirm the gate, run the build and watch check_table1.sh recompute the instrument-derived cells; if the build passes, those cells agree with the instruments. To confirm the hub-audited cells, there is nothing to run, and the record tells you that plainly rather than pretending otherwise. And to confirm the revision log, open the file and check the diff at the tag, the way the three reviewers did. What the incident log does not contain, and what remains missing from any attempt to fully reconstruct the story, is a way to audit the hub-supplied cells from the outside.

AB-S5-OK
